      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Race realists or proponents of population-level cognitive averages based on evolutionary timeframes might argue that the concept of evolutionary stability implies that certain cognitive traits have been favored in some populations because they provided a long-term survival advantage and, thus, became embedded in the gene pool over extended periods of time, even thousands of years.\n\nThey could cite studies which, they might claim, demonstrate consistent patterns of cognitive ability across different societies and epochs as evidence that these traits are not simply cultural or transient—they are deeply ingrained and seemingly evolutionary in nature. By using these timeframes, they make the argument that these differences are not simply the product of recent, reversible changes due to environmental factors but rather have been established and maintained throughout human evolutionary history, implying their biological root.\n\nProponents of this view would likely use these arguments to assert the permanence of certain cognitive traits within distinct racial groups, ostensibly secured through natural selection. They might contend that the heritability of cognitive traits across generations demonstrates their stability and enduring nature, rejecting the idea that social engineering, like improved education, could substantially alter genetically influenced intellectual capacity. This notion deters from the fact that genetic variability within a race and the increasing flow of information due to globalization and intermarriage muddy any clear-cut distinctions",
